<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1650">1650</a>–<a href="/years/1722">1722</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Duke of Marlborough, John Churchill</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough, was an English general and statesman who commanded the allied armies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. His victories at Blenheim in 1704, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Ramillies</a> in 1706, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oudenarde">Oudenarde</a> in 1708 and Malplaquet in 1709 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> a leading European power. His wife Sarah was a close friend of Queen Anne, but he fell from favour and was dismissed in 1711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough.jpg" alt="John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Michael Dahl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Churchill,_1st_Duke_of_Marlborough,_Captain-General_of_the_English_forces_and_Master-General_of_the_Ordnance,_1702_(c),_attributed_to_Michael_Dahl_91996.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Habsburgs Brabant became a main theatre of the Eighty Years' War, with the Spanish Fury at Antwerp in 1576, Alexander Farnese's siege of Antwerp in 1584–1585, and in 1706 the <b>Duke of Marlborough</b>'s victory at the Battle of Ramillies gave the allies control of the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By the early eighteenth century England was a leading naval and military power, and under the <b>Duke of Marlborough</b> its armies helped win the Battle of Blenheim in 1704.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Spanish Succession <b>John Churchill</b>, Duke of Marlborough, won at Ramillies in 1706, Oudenarde in 1708 and Malplaquet in 1709, and the Treaty of Utrecht (1713) gave Britain Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland and Nova Scotia and the asiento to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early modern period the principality sought safety in neutrality, but the armies of Louis XIV and his enemies repeatedly crossed its territory, and <b>Marlborough</b> captured the city and its citadel in 1702 during the War of the Spanish Succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a burlesque lament on the death of <b>John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough</b> (1650–1722), written on a false rumour of his death after the Battle of Malplaquet in 1709, the bloodiest battle of the War of the Spanish Succession.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Duke of Marlborough</b> and Prince Eugene of Savoy destroyed a Franco-Bavarian army at Blenheim.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Musbury, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Cumberland Lodge, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.75,-3.03&amp;year=1696" aria-label="Musbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4481 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.75,-3.03&amp;year=1696">What was here in 1696? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
